WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.995
&gt;&gt; For us it's
important to understand

00:00:04.995 --> 00:00:07.200
the Met is not just a place
that you visit.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.000
It's really a great service to
an audience all around the world.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.040
&gt;&gt; We believe we have
one objective that can inspire

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.735
every single person in the planet.

00:00:15.735 --> 00:00:17.820
How can we reduce
the distance between each of

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.105
those people and the object
will inspire them?

00:00:21.105 --> 00:00:24.900
&gt;&gt; In February 2017, the museum
made the decision to release

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.630
all the images and data of
our public domain artwork,

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:30.090
free of any copyright restriction.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.280
&gt;&gt; We are photographing all of

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.025
our two million objects
that the Met houses.

00:00:35.025 --> 00:00:37.320
Our task is really to
share our collection,

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.000
to disseminate our knowledge
to as many people as possible.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.590
&gt;&gt; Now, we want to answer
a question, using AI technologies,

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:45.075
what can people build on that?

00:00:45.075 --> 00:00:47.685
&gt;&gt; We put together
teams from the Met,

00:00:47.685 --> 00:00:52.360
Microsoft, MIT and we have
a hackathon happening.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.590
&gt;&gt; We can create experiences

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.660
around art that really
have never been seen

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:58.520
before because the technology we have

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.680
today has never been
available before.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.055
I'm helping mentor students
and teams that are building

00:01:04.055 --> 00:01:07.340
applications with the Microsoft
technology as a platform.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.830
It's incredible to see what
people are able to do.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.570
&gt;&gt; Artificial intelligence finds

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.345
patterns that the human eye
doesn't capture and we

00:01:13.345 --> 00:01:14.460
start actually learning about

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.015
ourselves better than we
probably knew already.

00:01:17.015 --> 00:01:19.520
&gt;&gt; One way that we
preserve culture is give

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.190
people tools to create
new things with it,

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:25.055
and to spread those creations
across the globe.

00:01:25.055 --> 00:01:26.465
&gt;&gt; As a museum experience

00:01:26.465 --> 00:01:28.010
starts becoming more
than just a visit to

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.420
a museum and looking at

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.430
the space of what we can
do in a virtual world,

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.260
the more people become
aware actually what's in

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.180
a met is relevant to me.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.290
Ultimately, it's those ideas we

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:37.490
want people to take away with them.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:38.945
It's the connections to culture,

00:01:38.945 --> 00:01:41.190
the connections to history

